/* ==============================================================
   04 — MoveLin: the straight line
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   Linear motion, and why it is not the default
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted
   WATCH     Same start and end as example 03, different path
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE COMMAND

       MoveLin(x, y, z, γ)

   Identical arguments to MovePose. Completely different promise.

   MovePose says: end up at this pose.
   MoveLin says:  end up at this pose, AND make the tool tip
                  travel there in a straight line, turning
                  smoothly as it goes.

   That guarantee is what you need whenever the path is part of
   the job:

     - sliding a part along a fixture or into a nest
     - pushing a connector or a pin home sideways
     - dispensing a bead of adhesive along an edge
     - any move close to something that a curved path would hit

   WHAT IT COSTS

   A straight line in space is a complicated thing in joint
   space — joints 1, 2 and 4 have to be continuously coordinated
   to keep the tip on the line. Three consequences, all worth
   knowing before a customer finds them:

   1. IT IS SLOWER. Speed is bounded by the joint that is working
      hardest at the tightest point of the path. A joint move
      between the same two points is usually faster.

   2. IT CAN BE REFUSED. If any point along the line is out of
      reach — further out than the arm's 225 mm, closer to the
      base than the elbow can fold, past a joint limit — or if
      the tool would have to turn exactly 180 degrees, the robot
      rejects the command and DOES NOT START MOVING. That is the
      right behaviour: you get an error, not a robot that stops
      halfway along a bead. Note the difference from a joint
      move, which only cares about the two ends.

   3. IT HOLDS THE ARM POSTURE. The elbow stays bent the same
      way — lefty or righty — for the whole line. The manual puts
      it bluntly: it is physically impossible to follow a linear
      path while changing configuration. See 02_intermediate/04.

   THE RULE

   Use MoveLin only where the straight line is genuinely
   required. Use MoveJoints or MovePose for everything else —
   and on a SCARA, MoveJump for the classic up-over-down hop (see
   02_intermediate/07). This is the single most common mistake in
   a first robot program: making every move linear, then
   wondering why the cycle time is poor and why the robot
   occasionally refuses a position it can plainly reach.

   Note that the speed for these moves is set in mm/s with
   SetCartLinVel — a real velocity, not a percentage. See
   06_linear_motion_settings.mxprog.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(15)
SetCartLinVel(40)          // 40 mm/s. Slow enough to watch.

// --- Start from the zero position -----------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- The same points as example 03 ----------------------------
// Move into the starting pose. This first one is a MovePose,
// because getting into position is repositioning, not path work.
MovePose(170, 60, 59, 0)

Delay(1)

// This is the comparison that sells it. In 03_move_pose.mxprog
// the equivalent move bowed outward. Here the tip runs dead
// straight across and up — and the arm has to work for it:
// watch joints 1, 2 and 4 all keep changing speed to hold the
// line.
MoveLin(170, -60, 109, 0)

Delay(1)

// Straight back across in y. Sight down the line and it holds.
MoveLin(170, 60, 109, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- The approach-and-retract pattern -------------------------
// This is the shape of nearly every real pick or place, and it
// is worth showing on its own.

// 1. Get above the target. Fast, path irrelevant, so MovePose.
MovePose(180, 0, 100, 0)

// 2. Descend straight down onto it. Path critical, so MoveLin.
MoveLin(180, 0, 60, 0)

// 3. Slide 30 mm in towards the base, along the bench — pushing
//    a part into a nest, say. A horizontal straight line: the
//    case where MoveLin earns its keep on a SCARA.
MoveLin(150, 0, 60, 0)

// 4. (A tool would grip or release here.)
Delay(0.5)

// 5. Lift straight back up before going anywhere. Skipping this
//    is how parts get dragged out of fixtures.
MoveLin(150, 0, 100, 0)


/* A SCARA footnote on step 2.

   On this robot a purely vertical move is joint 3 alone, so even
   a MovePose straight down would happen to travel in a straight
   line. MoveLin is still the right command there: it states the
   intent, and it keeps the line straight if someone later edits
   the target so that x, y or γ change too. The slide in step 3
   is where the difference becomes physical — a MovePose there
   would curve. */


// --- Back to zero ---------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run 03 and 04 back to back. Same endpoints, visibly
      different paths.

   2. Change step 3 to a MovePose and watch the slide bow. On a
      bench with a real nest, that bow is a part knocked out of
      its pocket.

   3. Note that steps 2 and 5 are really "go down 40 mm from
      here" and "go back up 40 mm". Writing absolute coordinates
      for that is fragile. There are better ways: MoveLinRelWrf,
      in 02_intermediate/03, and MoveJump, which does the whole
      up-over-down in one command, in 02_intermediate/07.

   NEXT   05_joint_motion_settings.mxprog — controlling speed.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
